With the second beta of <a href="https://www.macrumors.com/roundup/macos-27/">macOS Golden Gate</a> 27.2, Apple updated the look of the volume and brightness sliders when you adjust them with the keyboard.<br/>
<br/>
<img src="https://images.macrumors.com/article-new/2026/09/macos-27-2-new-sliders.jpg" alt="" width="2000" height="1125" class="aligncenter size-full wp-image-1066568" /><br/>
The new sliders have a segmented bar instead of a plain line, making it clearer how much each key press adjusts volume or brightness. Apple used a similar design in macOS Sequoia before Liquid Glass, and the new look is a cross between the old slider and the Liquid Glass slider.<br/>
<br/>
There are no longer two icons at the beginning and end of the slider, and Apple now uses a single larger icon on the left. The sliders appear where a matching menu bar icon is located, so if you have a dedicated sound icon, the slider shows up below it.<br/>
<br/>
<div class="center-wrap"><blockquote class="twitter-tweet"><p lang="en" dir="ltr">New in macOS 27.2 beta 2: There is a new UI when changing your volume/brightness with your keyboard <a href="https://t.co/GpwerF1QL7">pic.twitter.com/GpwerF1QL7</a></p>&mdash; Aaron (@aaronp613) <a href="https://x.com/aaronp613/status/2102156914524070164?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw">September 21, 2026</a></blockquote> <script async src="https://platform.x.com/widgets.js" charset="utf-8"></script></div><br/>
The design of the Control Center sliders did not change, and the updated look can only be seen when you change volume or brightness with the keyboard.<br/>
